Don't give every thread of the host process a 256 KiB signal stack.

Zig std's `Thread.maybeAttachSignalStack` keeps its alternate signal stack
in a `threadlocal [std.options.signal_stack_size]u8` (1 << 18 by default).
Linked into a host program, that lands in the program's static TLS, which
glibc allocates and zeroes for every thread the host starts, not only for
threads Zig starts. illogical measured about 260 KB of RSS per thread from
it (S9), 1.3 MB per pane. libghostty-vt starts no threads, so nothing uses
the stack. With the option null, `.tbss` goes from 0x402c0 to 0x1b0 bytes.

illogical applies this at build time (vendor/libghostty-vt-sys/build.rs)
until Ghostty has an equivalent.

--- a/src/lib_vt.zig
+++ b/src/lib_vt.zig
@@ -376,6 +376,11 @@
 pub const std_options: std.Options = opts: {
     var options: std.Options = .{};
 
+    // No threadlocal signal stack: as a library, static TLS is allocated
+    // (and zeroed) for every thread of the host process, and libghostty-vt
+    // starts no threads that would use it.
+    options.signal_stack_size = null;
+
     if (builtin.target.cpu.arch.isWasm()) {
         // Wasm builds we specifically want to optimize for space with small
         // releases so we bump up to warn. Everything else acts pretty normal.
